  "_codes_comment": "Next release (design §2.10, PR 2). codes is the one list of codes shared by the three SDKs and the HTTP API, in this order: the API's, then the SDK's own (unreachable onward). Per code: the class an SDK raises before sending (null: never) and for the core's answer (null: never); the HTTP status the core usually answers with (informative, not checked); the fix, one clause; the docs anchor. Class names are the same in the three SDKs: WakeshiftError is the base (TS, Python; Go has none); APIError; APIConnectionError, an APIError with no status; IdReusedError, an APIError; UsageError (Python: also a ValueError). Go has *APIError (Status 0 for APIConnectionError's codes, IsCode for id_reused) and *UsageError. TypeScript's isWakeshiftError(err, code) narrows to the classes listed for that code; its code parameter accepts exactly these codes. TypeScript KnownCode, Python ErrorCode and Go's Code constants (CodeBadRequest, …, CodeIDReused, …) list exactly these codes. Loaders: check the SDK's list equals this one, and that every class an SDK raises for a code is listed here. A code's fix_python, where given, is the fix as the Python SDK says it, with Python's names (define_event, resolve_session, raise); the others say fix.",
  "messages": {
    "_comment": "Next release (design §2.10 'Messages say what failed, the fix in one clause, and a docs anchor'). Every message the SDK writes itself (every UsageError, unreachable and timeout) has the format below and contains the code's fix from codes verbatim. Messages from the core's answer keep responses[].want.text unchanged in this release. No message contains the key or any fragment or length of it, a session key, a caller's token or header, a signing secret, a publish URL token, a payload, tool arguments, or the vendor's own error message (for identify and authorize, only the error's type).",
    "format": "wakeshift: {what}. {fix} {anchor}",
    "checks": [
      "starts with \"wakeshift: \"",
      "contains the code's fix verbatim",
      "ends with \" \" and the code's anchor"
    ],
    "unreachable": "unreachable.message_prefix, the transport error, unreachable.message_suffix, then \". \" and the anchor: can't reach Wakeshift at {core} (connection refused). Check that WAKESHIFT_CORE, or the core option, is the address of the console your key came from. https://wakeshift.ai/docs/errors#unreachable",
    "timeout": "wakeshift: Wakeshift at {core} didn't answer in time. The outcome is unknown; an emit sent again with the same id emits once. https://wakeshift.ai/docs/errors#timeout",
    "example": "wakeshift: start_crawl subscribed to crawl.finished, which isn't in its subscribes. Add the event to the tool's subscribes. https://wakeshift.ai/docs/errors#undeclared_subscribe"
  },
  "client_checks_next": {
    "_comment": "Next release (PR 2, PR 5, PR 7). Every client_checks case above raises UsageError with the same code and no status (status applies to today's SDKs only), its message containing says and following messages. The cases below are new; same events and rules as client_checks (game.turn personal, table.news shared), run inside a tool that subscribes declaring table.news only, unless the case says otherwise. subscribe-options.json replay_bounds and disabled-client.json add more.",
    "cases": [
      {
        "op": "subscribe_undeclared",
        "name": "subscribe: an event the tool didn't declare (game.turn)",
        "code": "undeclared_subscribe",
        "says": "isn't in its subscribes",
        "note": "TypeScript registerTool also refuses it at compile time; the run-time check holds on every path, wrap included"
      },
      {
        "op": "current_call_outside",
        "name": "currentCall outside any tool call, enabled client",
        "code": "no_call",
        "says": "outside a tool call",
        "skip": {
          "go": "ToolCallFrom(ctx) returns nil; subscribe_nil_call covers Go"
        }
      },
      {
        "op": "subscribe_nil_call",
        "name": "Go: Subscribe with a nil call (ToolCallFrom outside a call)",
        "code": "no_call",
        "says": "outside a tool call",
        "skip": {
          "typescript": "Go only",
          "python": "Go only"
        }
      },
      {
        "op": "subscribe_after_return",
        "name": "subscribe on a call whose handler already returned",
        "code": "call_ended",
        "says": "already returned"
      },
      {
        "op": "identify_throws",
        "name": "subscribe when identify throws (Go: returns an error)",
        "code": "identify_failed",
        "says": "identify",
        "must_not_contain": "boom-canary",
        "note": "identify throws an error whose message is boom-canary; the vendor's tool code still runs"
      },
      {
        "op": "identify_times_out",
        "name": "subscribe when identify takes over 10 seconds",
        "code": "identify_failed",
        "says": "identify",
        "note": "use a fake clock or the SDK's test hook; don't sleep 10 seconds"
      },
      {
        "op": "require_user_anonymous",
        "name": "subscribe with requireUser and an identify that returns no user",
        "code": "user_required",
        "says": "identify gave no user"
      }
    ]
  }
